// A General MIDI instrument bank for opl_midi.fc, written by hand.
//
// A MIDI file names its instruments and does not describe them: program 40 is
// "Violin", and what a violin *is* has to come from somewhere else. On real
// hardware that somewhere was the driver's bank file. This is ours — sixty
// two-operator voices and the two tables that point all 128 GM programs and
// the whole percussion map at them.
//
// It is checked in rather than loaded so the demos have a voice out of the
// box with no licensing question attached: nearly every OPL bank in
// circulation was extracted from a commercial game. `opl_midi.load_bank`
// reads those formats for anyone who has one and wants to swap it in.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reading a patch
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A voice is two operators, a modulator and a carrier. In FM connection
// (`n_conn` bit 0 clear) the modulator bends the carrier's phase, so:
//
//   MULT (low nibble of `*_char`) is the frequency ratio between the two, and
//     it picks the timbre more than anything else does. 1:1 with feedback is
//     a sawtooth, 2:1 is hollow and woody, 3:1 and up go metallic and bell-
//     like, and a large ratio with no harmonic relation reads as noise.
//   TL (low six bits of `*_scale`) counts *down* — 0 is loudest, 63 silent.
//     On the carrier it is loudness; on the modulator it is modulation depth,
//     which the ear hears as brightness.
//   EGT (bit 5 of `*_char`) decides whether a held note sustains (organ,
//     strings, brass) or decays on its own regardless (piano, guitar, bell).
//     It is the single bit that most determines what family a patch belongs
//     to, and getting it wrong makes a string section sound like a marimba.
//   AR/DR/SL/RR (`*_atk` and `*_sus`, a nibble each) are the envelope, rates
//     0..15 with higher being faster. SL is a level, not a rate, and 15 is
//     special: it decays to silence rather than holding.
//   FB (bits 3..1 of `n_conn`) feeds the modulator back into itself, which
//     on a 1:1 pair is what turns a sine into a saw.
//
// Additive connection (`n_conn` bit 0 set) sums both operators instead, which
// makes fuller, flatter voices — organs and pads — and costs the FM
// brightness. `opl_midi` scales both operators' levels in that mode and only
// the carrier's in FM, so either kind responds correctly to velocity.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two rules, both learned the hard way
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A General MIDI file names a program and expects it to arrive at a sensible
// level. Nothing enforces that but the bank, and getting it wrong does not
// look like a bug — it looks like the music being badly mixed. Both rules
// below came out of measuring this bank with `tools/bank_probe.fc`, and both
// apply to any bank, not just this one.
//
// **1. SL is a level, not a rate.** A percussive voice (EGT clear) runs
// attack → decay-to-SL → release, so SL is how far the note falls *before*
// the release rate gets a say, and it falls that far as fast as DR allows.
// SL 7 throws away 21 dB in the first tenth of a second; SL 11 throws away 33
// dB in ten milliseconds. What is left is a high peak with almost no energy
// in it — a patch that measures loud on a meter reading peaks and disappears
// in a mix. Twenty-five patches here had exactly that, one of them 24 dB
// under the rest of its kit. The fix is the same every time: **SL 0, and put
// the length in RR**, which gives a plain exponential decay — what a struck
// or plucked string does. DR then shapes only the first instant.
//
// **2. Level-match the patches, because velocity cannot.** Before this bank
// was measured its melodic voices spanned 23 dB at the same velocity, so a
// program change was a volume change and no file could sound balanced through
// it. They now sit inside about 4 dB (middle 80%), which is what makes an
// arbitrary file play at a sensible balance without editing. Percussion sits
// a couple of decibels under the melodic voices, and the long metal — crash,
// ride, open hat — three under that again.
//
// The exception worth knowing: the melodic-voice noise fakes (snare, hats,
// cymbals, claps, patches 44–59) are 8–12 dB quieter than everything else and
// cannot be brought up. They make their noise with a large inharmonic MULT on
// a pulse-sine with feedback, which spreads their energy thinly across the
// whole spectrum, and their carriers are already at TL 0. Raising their
// modulation depth buys 2 dB, moving them down four octaves buys 4 dB, and
// neither is worth the character it spends — both were tried and measured.
//
// The real answer is the `rhythm_kit` below, and it is not a level fix at all:
// the OPL2 *does* have a noise generator, in rhythm mode, and a voice driven
// by it is noise rather than an impression of noise. Measured flatness 0.81
// against the fake's 0.49 for a snare, 1.0 being white. The five rhythm
// voices are level-matched to within 0.4 dB of each other and sit two
// decibels above the melodic drum median. What stays on melodic voices is
// what rhythm mode has no voice for — congas, cowbell, tambourine, woodblock
// — and those are the quiet ones the paragraph above is about.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// How complete this is
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Sixty voices across 175 GM slots means families share. Every one of the
// sixteen melodic families has three or four distinct voices and each program
// points at the nearest, so nothing is silent and nothing is wildly wrong,
// but a bassoon and an oboe are the same patch and a splash cymbal is a
// crash. Percussion notes outside the standard 35..81 map are silent.
//
// Three of the melodic voices — celesta, music box and the plucked bass —
// began as Fuzzel Fobble's own hand-tuned game instruments, and have since
// been relevelled to sit in a bank rather than to play alone.

namespace shared::

// opl_midi is a sibling in `shared::`, so it needs no import here.

// A hand-authored General MIDI bank: sixty OPL2 voices indexed across all 128
// melodic programs and the 35..81 percussion map.
//
// ```fc
// let kit = opl_bank_gm.make()
// let p = opl_midi.init(44100, tune, kit)
// ```
//
// `make` allocates, so a bank it returns is released with
// `opl_midi.free_bank`. See the file header for how to read a patch and for
// what sixty voices across 175 slots does and does not cover.
module opl_bank_gm =

    // ============================================================
    // Voice names
    // ============================================================

    // Indices into the patch table below. Naming them is what lets the two
    // index tables read as instrument assignments rather than as walls of
    // numbers — and what makes a mistake in them visible.
    private let piano    = 0
    private let epiano   = 1
    private let harpsi   = 2
    private let clav     = 3
    private let celesta  = 4
    private let mbox     = 5
    private let vibes    = 6
    private let marimba  = 7
    private let bells    = 8
    private let organ    = 9
    private let percorg  = 10
    private let pipeorg  = 11
    private let nylon    = 12
    private let steel    = 13
    private let dist     = 14
    private let acbass   = 15
    private let elbass   = 16
    private let synbass  = 17
    private let strings  = 18
    private let cello    = 19
    private let pizz     = 20
    private let harp     = 21
    private let ensemble = 22
    private let choir    = 23
    private let synstr   = 24
    private let trumpet  = 25
    private let tbone    = 26
    private let brass    = 27
    private let sax      = 28
    private let clar     = 29
    private let oboe     = 30
    private let flute    = 31
    private let recdr    = 32
    private let panfl    = 33
    private let sqrlead  = 34
    private let sawlead  = 35
    private let callio   = 36
    private let warmpad  = 37
    private let brtpad   = 38
    private let fxswp    = 39
    private let ethnic   = 40
    private let tperc    = 41
    private let noise    = 42
    private let timp     = 43
    // Percussion.
    private let kick     = 44
    private let snare    = 45
    private let rimshot  = 46
    private let tom_lo   = 47
    private let tom_mid  = 48
    private let tom_hi   = 49
    private let hat_shut = 50
    private let hat_open = 51
    private let crash    = 52
    private let ride     = 53
    private let clap     = 54
    private let cowbell  = 55
    private let woodblk  = 56
    private let tamb     = 57
    private let conga    = 58
    private let ptick    = 59

    // ============================================================
    // Percussion note map
    // ============================================================

    // (note, voice) pairs covering General MIDI's percussion key map, notes
    // 35..81. Notes outside it are left silent rather than pointed at a
    // catch-all: a file reaching past the standard map is doing something
    // this bank has no opinion about, and a wrong drum is worse than none.
    //
    // Several notes deliberately share a voice — the three crash and splash
    // cymbals are one crash, the bongos and congas and timbales are one hand
    // drum. Sixty voices do not stretch to a full drum kit, and the notes
    // that share are the ones a listener is least likely to hear as wrong.
    private let drum_map = i32[94] {
        35, kick,       36, kick,       37, rimshot,    38, snare,
        39, clap,       40, snare,      41, tom_lo,     42, hat_shut,
        43, tom_lo,     44, hat_shut,   45, tom_lo,     46, hat_open,
        47, tom_mid,    48, tom_mid,    49, crash,      50, tom_hi,
        51, ride,       52, crash,      53, ride,       54, tamb,
        55, crash,      56, cowbell,    57, crash,      58, tamb,
        59, ride,       60, conga,      61, conga,      62, conga,
        63, conga,      64, conga,      65, conga,      66, conga,
        67, cowbell,    68, cowbell,    69, hat_shut,   70, hat_shut,
        71, ptick,      72, ptick,      73, ptick,      74, ptick,
        75, woodblk,    76, woodblk,    77, woodblk,    78, ptick,
        79, ptick,      80, ptick,      81, ptick }

    // ============================================================
    // Rhythm-mode kit
    // ============================================================

    // Which of rhythm mode's five voices each percussion note plays on, as
    // (note, voice) pairs. Voices are 0 = bass drum, 1 = snare, 2 = tom,
    // 3 = cymbal, 4 = hi-hat, matching the order of `rhythm_kit` below.
    //
    // Deliberately short. Rhythm mode does the core kit with the chip's real
    // noise generator and does nothing else at all: there is one hi-hat, so
    // routing the open hat here would make it identical to the closed one,
    // and there is no cowbell, conga, tambourine or woodblock whatever.
    // Every note not named here keeps the melodic voice it had, which is why
    // the two paths coexist instead of competing.
    private let rhythm_notes = i32[30] {
        35, 0,   36, 0,                         // bass drum
        38, 1,   40, 1,                         // snare
        41, 2,   43, 2,   45, 2,                // toms, low to high
        47, 2,   48, 2,   50, 2,
        42, 4,   44, 4,                         // closed and pedal hi-hat
        46, 5,                                  // open hi-hat: same operator
        49, 3,   57, 3 }                        // crash 1 and 2

    // The five voices themselves, in voice order. Four of them are single
    // operators and use only the modulator half of a `patch`; the bass drum
    // is a whole two-operator channel and uses all of it.
    //
    // Every one has SL 0 — the length is in RR, per the rule above — and
    // waveform 0. A rhythm voice does not need a clever waveform to sound
    // noisy, because its noise does not come from the waveform: the snare,
    // cymbal and hi-hat get theirs from the chip's noise register, which is
    // the whole reason to be here. Measured spectral flatness against the
    // melodic-voice fakes they replace: snare 0.49 -> 0.81, hi-hat
    // 0.49 -> 0.72, where 1.0 is white noise.
    private let rhythm_kit = () ->
        alloc(opl_midi.patch[6] {
            // Bass drum. A 0.5x pair with feedback and a soft modulator
            // attack — the thump is the envelope, since the chip cannot
            // sweep pitch down the way a real kick does.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x00, m_scale = 0x0A, m_atk = 0xA8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
                c_char = 0x00, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF8, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x0C, fixed_note = 36 },
            // Snare. Its noise is the register's; MULT 12 only decides how
            // much tone sits under it.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x0C, m_scale = 0x00, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x00 },
            // Tom. The one rhythm voice with a pitch — `strike_rhythm`
            // retunes channel 8 per note, so General MIDI's six toms really
            // are six pitches. An octave down puts them where a tom lives.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x02, m_scale = 0x00, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x07, m_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x00, transpose = -12, fixed_note = 48 },
            // Crash. The long one: RR 5 is about a second and a half.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x00, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x05, m_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x00 },
            // Closed hi-hat. Short, and its `fixed_note` tunes channel 7 for
            // the snare as well as itself.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x00, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x09, m_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x00, fixed_note = 84 },
            // Open hi-hat: the closed one's operator with a release six
            // times longer. Rhythm mode has a single hi-hat, so this is the
            // only way the two can differ — and the alternative, leaving the
            // open hat on a melodic voice, left it nine decibels under the
            // rest of the kit with nowhere to go.
            opl_midi.patch {
                m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x00, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x06, m_wave = 0x00,
                n_conn = 0x00, fixed_note = 84 } })!

    // ============================================================
    // Program map
    // ============================================================

    // Every General MIDI program, 0-based as the wire format numbers them —
    // so program 0 is the Acoustic Grand Piano that a printed GM chart calls
    // number 1.
    private let program_map = i32[128] {
        // 0-7    pianos
        piano, piano, piano, piano, epiano, epiano, harpsi, clav,
        // 8-15   chromatic percussion
        celesta, celesta, mbox, vibes, marimba, marimba, bells, marimba,
        // 16-23  organ
        organ, percorg, percorg, pipeorg, pipeorg, pipeorg, pipeorg, pipeorg,
        // 24-31  guitar
        nylon, steel, steel, steel, steel, dist, dist, nylon,
        // 32-39  bass
        acbass, elbass, elbass, acbass, synbass, synbass, synbass, synbass,
        // 40-47  solo strings
        strings, strings, cello, cello, strings, pizz, harp, timp,
        // 48-55  ensemble
        ensemble, ensemble, synstr, synstr, choir, choir, choir, brass,
        // 56-63  brass
        trumpet, tbone, tbone, trumpet, tbone, brass, brass, brass,
        // 64-71  reed
        sax, sax, sax, sax, oboe, oboe, oboe, clar,
        // 72-79  pipe
        recdr, flute, recdr, panfl, panfl, panfl, recdr, flute,
        // 80-87  synth lead
        sqrlead, sawlead, callio, callio, sawlead, callio, sawlead, sawlead,
        // 88-95  synth pad
        warmpad, warmpad, brtpad, warmpad, warmpad, brtpad, warmpad, brtpad,
        // 96-103 synth effects
        fxswp, fxswp, fxswp, fxswp, fxswp, fxswp, fxswp, fxswp,
        // 104-111 ethnic
        ethnic, ethnic, ethnic, ethnic, marimba, oboe, strings, oboe,
        // 112-119 percussive
        bells, tperc, tperc, tperc, tperc, tperc, tperc, noise,
        // 120-127 sound effects
        noise, noise, noise, noise, noise, noise, noise, noise }

    // ============================================================
    // The voices
    // ============================================================

    // Build the bank. Allocates; release it with `opl_midi.free_bank`.
    //
    // The patch table is built here rather than held as module data because a
    // bank owns its three slices — see `opl_midi.bank` — and one ownership
    // rule for both authored and loaded banks is worth one small startup
    // allocation.
    let make = () ->
        let ps = alloc(opl_midi.patch[60] {

        // ---------- 0-3  pianos ----------

        // Acoustic piano — the patch every file that names no instrument
        // arrives on, so it carries more music than any other here.
        //
        // A struck string does two things at once, and a piano is the
        // instrument where both are obvious. It *decays*, from the moment the
        // hammer leaves it. And it *darkens* while it decays: the high
        // partials the hammer put there die away far faster than the
        // fundamental, so the note starts bright and ends round. An
        // instrument that decays without darkening is a plucked one — which
        // is exactly what this patch used to be, and why an unlabelled MIDI
        // file came out sounding like a harpsichord.
        //
        // Each half of the pair answers one of those:
        //
        //   The **carrier** is percussive (EGT clear) and plainly
        //     exponential — SL 0 and the length in RR, per the sustain-level
        //     rule above. At C4 it is 20 dB down after 1.3 s. The old patch
        //     was 0.9 dB down after half a second, which is not a decay at
        //     all: a note held its level until the key came up, and a piece
        //     of two-part counterpoint turned into a drone.
        //   The **modulator** is the one operator here that *sustains*
        //     (EGT set), which looks backwards and is the whole trick. It
        //     attacks to a deep index, falls 18 dB in about 150 ms, and then
        //     holds — so brightness drops sharply and settles at a floor
        //     rather than collapsing to a sine. Measured across harmonics
        //     3-8 against the fundamental, the note darkens 14 dB in half a
        //     second and stays there. Let the modulator decay to nothing
        //     instead (EGT clear on both) and the tail is a pure tone, which
        //     reads as a flute rather than a piano.
        //
        // **KSR on both** is what gives the keyboard its shape: envelope
        // rates scale with the key, so C2 rings for 2.8 s, C4 for 1.3 s and
        // C6 for 0.7 s — a bass:treble ratio of four, against the old
        // patch's 1.2. A real grand is steeper still, but one bit of key
        // scaling cannot buy more, and four is the difference between a
        // keyboard and a rank of identical pipes.
        //
        // Feedback comes down from 5 to 4. Feedback is not enveloped, so on
        // the old patch it was a sawtooth held for the length of the note;
        // the strike's bite now comes from the modulator's own attack, which
        // goes away like a hammer does.
        //
        // Two things were measured and rejected. A **two-stage carrier** —
        // fast prompt decay to SL, slow aftersound on RR, which is what a
        // real string does — costs 8 dB of everything for it, exactly as
        // rule 1 above predicts. And a **detuned second voice** for the
        // unison shimmer of two or three strings: a real grand's unisons sit
        // within a cent or so, which at C4 is a beat once every two seconds
        // — slower than the note lasts. Detune it far enough to hear and you
        // have written the honky-tonk two programs along.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x31, m_scale = 0x46, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x66, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x11, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x02, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x08 },
        // Electric piano. 2:1 and a half-sine modulator give the tine its
        // hollow knock; slower attack and a longer tail than the acoustic.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x02, m_scale = 0x54, m_atk = 0xE3, m_sus = 0x34, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x02, c_atk = 0xE2, c_sus = 0x25, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06 },
        // Harpsichord. Instant attack, half-sine on both operators for the
        // plectrum's buzz. Its sustain level used to be 7 — a 21 dB drop
        // inside the first tenth of a second — which made an eighth note
        // almost all attack and no note, and buried the part in any mix that
        // had something sustaining in the same register. SL 0 and RR 6 let a
        // plucked string ring the way one does, and leave the clavinet below
        // as the short, rude member of the family.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x45, m_atk = 0xF3, m_sus = 0x07, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x06, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x0C },
        // Clavinet. Maximum feedback on a 1:1 pair, very short — the
        // percussive, almost rude end of the plucked-string family.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x43, m_atk = 0xF7, m_sus = 0x09, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x08, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0E },

        // ---------- 4-8  chromatic percussion ----------

        // Celesta — from Fuzzel Fobble's `ins_chime`, opened up for melodic
        // use. Glassy, and it rings: as a game chime it decayed 9 dB before
        // it had finished starting and was gone in a tenth of a second,
        // which is a blip rather than a note.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x14, m_scale = 0x9A, m_atk = 0xF7, m_sus = 0x07, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x11, c_scale = 0x40, c_atk = 0xF6, c_sus = 0x06, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Music box — Fuzzel Fobble's `ins_music_box`, relevelled. A 3:1
        // modulator gives the inharmonic ring of a struck metal bar, and
        // both operators are percussive so a note blooms and fades on its
        // own with nobody having to release it.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x03, m_scale = 0x53, m_atk = 0xF4, m_sus = 0x36, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x36, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Vibraphone. 4:1 and the AM bit on both operators, which is where
        // the family's tremolo comes from; longer decay than the music box.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x94, m_scale = 0x53, m_atk = 0xF3, m_sus = 0x26, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x81, c_scale = 0x01, c_atk = 0xF2, c_sus = 0x25, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Marimba. 3:1 but with a fast decay and no ring, which is what
        // reads as wood rather than metal.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x03, m_scale = 0x4E, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Tubular bell. 7:1 is deliberately inharmonic, and the very slow
        // decay is the whole character — this one rings for seconds.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x07, m_scale = 0x52, m_atk = 0xF2, m_sus = 0x14, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x01, c_atk = 0xF1, c_sus = 0x13, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },

        // ---------- 9-11  organ ----------

        // Drawbar organ. Additive rather than FM, EGT set on both operators
        // and SL 0, so the note holds dead flat for as long as the key is
        // down and stops the instant it isn't. That envelope *is* the organ.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x22, m_atk = 0xF0, m_sus = 0x0F, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x22, c_scale = 0x09, c_atk = 0xF0, c_sus = 0x0F, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x01 },
        // Percussive organ. The same flat sustain with a decaying attack
        // transient on top, which is the drawbar's percussion stop.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x22, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x2F, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x09, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x0F, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x01 },
        // Pipe organ. A 1:4 pair sounding two octaves apart, which is what
        // an open diapason with a mixture stop drawn does.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x31, m_scale = 0x22, m_atk = 0xF0, m_sus = 0x0F, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x24, c_scale = 0x09, c_atk = 0xF0, c_sus = 0x0F, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x01 },

        // ---------- 12-14  guitar ----------

        // Nylon guitar. Gentle pluck, moderate feedback, medium decay.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x4C, m_atk = 0xF4, m_sus = 0x46, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x02, c_atk = 0xF3, c_sus = 0x36, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06 },
        // Steel guitar. Brighter modulator and a half-sine give the string
        // its metal; faster attack than the nylon.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x47, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x55, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x45, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0A },
        // Overdriven guitar. Full feedback, half-sine on both, and EGT set
        // so the note sustains under the key the way a driven amp does.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x45, m_atk = 0xF3, m_sus = 0x2F, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x01, c_atk = 0xF2, c_sus = 0x1F, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x0E },

        // ---------- 15-17  bass ----------

        // Acoustic bass — Fuzzel Fobble's `ins_bass`. Feedback on a 1:1 pair
        // turns the carrier's sine into a saw, which is the round, woody
        // bottom end this wants. Plucked, medium decay. Its carrier TL is
        // the one thing changed from the original: 11 was right for a voice
        // that played alone in a game, and a bass eight decibels under
        // everything else in a bank reads as missing rather than as quiet.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x18, m_atk = 0xF4, m_sus = 0x36, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF3, c_sus = 0x26, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0A },
        // Electric bass. The same idea run brighter and shorter — more
        // finger noise, less body.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x14, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x46, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x37, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0A },
        // Synth bass. EGT set so it holds flat under the key, and a
        // half-sine carrier for the squared-off edge.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x10, m_atk = 0xF7, m_sus = 0x1F, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x05, c_atk = 0xF6, c_sus = 0x0F, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x08 },

        // ---------- 18-21  solo strings ----------

        // Solo strings. Slow attack, sustaining, and the VIB bit on both
        // operators — the chip's own vibrato is close enough to a player's
        // that a bowed line does not need one written into the file.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1B, m_atk = 0x63, m_sus = 0x0C, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x61, c_scale = 0x08, c_atk = 0x74, c_sus = 0x0C, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Cello. Darker and slower off the mark than the violin above.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1D, m_atk = 0x53, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x06, c_atk = 0x64, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Pizzicato strings. Same family, opposite envelope: instant attack
        // and an immediate decay, EGT clear.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x02, m_scale = 0x4B, m_atk = 0xF9, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF8, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x02 },
        // Harp. A pizzicato with a much longer tail and no bite.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x02, m_scale = 0x52, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x35, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x25, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },

        // ---------- 22-24  ensemble ----------

        // String ensemble. Slower and quieter than the solo strings, and
        // additive — a section is many instruments summing, not one being
        // modulated, and additive is the closer analogy.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x20, m_atk = 0x42, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x61, c_scale = 0x08, c_atk = 0x53, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x01 },
        // Choir. An abs-sine modulator adds the even harmonics a vowel needs
        // without the buzz a half-sine would bring.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1C, m_atk = 0x62, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x02,
            c_char = 0x61, c_scale = 0x06, c_atk = 0x63, c_sus = 0x0A, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Synth strings. Brighter and more even than the acoustic section.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1A, m_atk = 0x52, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x05, c_atk = 0x63, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x01 },

        // ---------- 25-27  brass ----------

        // Trumpet. Brass is the one family where the *attack rate* carries
        // the identity: a lip takes a moment to speak, so AR sits around 10
        // rather than 15, and the modulator arrives fractionally ahead of
        // the carrier so the note brightens as it starts.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x1A, m_atk = 0xA3, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x07, c_atk = 0xB4, c_sus = 0x0C, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0A },
        // Trombone and tuba. The same shape, darker and slower still.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x1C, m_atk = 0x93, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x06, c_atk = 0xA4, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x08 },
        // Brass section. Faster and louder than either — a section attacks
        // together and so hides the individual lip.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x18, m_atk = 0xC4, m_sus = 0x0C, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x09, c_atk = 0xC5, c_sus = 0x0D, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0A },

        // ---------- 28-30  reed ----------

        // Saxophone. 1:2 with vibrato and moderate feedback — reedier than
        // the clarinet, breathier than the oboe.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1B, m_atk = 0x93, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x62, c_scale = 0x06, c_atk = 0xA4, c_sus = 0x0C, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06 },
        // Clarinet. 2:1 with no feedback at all, which is the classic FM
        // recipe for a closed pipe: odd harmonics only, and hollow.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x22, m_scale = 0x1F, m_atk = 0xB2, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x09, c_atk = 0xC3, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Oboe and bassoon. 3:1 is what makes it nasal, and the half-sine
        // carrier sharpens it further.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x23, m_scale = 0x1A, m_atk = 0xA3, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x04, c_atk = 0xB3, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x04 },

        // ---------- 31-33  pipe ----------

        // Flute. Very deep modulator TL, so there is barely any modulation
        // at all — near a pure sine, which is very nearly what a flute is.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x2D, m_atk = 0x83, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x08, c_atk = 0x94, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Recorder and piccolo. Brighter and quicker to speak.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x27, m_atk = 0xA3, m_sus = 0x0B, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x07, c_atk = 0xB4, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Pan flute. The breathiest of the three: slow attack, vibrato on
        // both operators, quiet.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x2A, m_atk = 0x73, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x61, c_scale = 0x07, c_atk = 0x84, c_sus = 0x0B, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },

        // ---------- 34-36  synth lead ----------

        // Square lead. Half-sine on both operators and a flat sustain.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x18, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x0F, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x0E, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Saw lead. 1:1 with maximum feedback, which *is* a sawtooth.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x14, m_atk = 0xF5, m_sus = 0x0E, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x0A, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x0D, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x0E },
        // Calliope and the softer leads. Gentler attack, less modulation.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x21, m_scale = 0x22, m_atk = 0xB4, m_sus = 0x0C, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x05, c_atk = 0xC4, c_sus = 0x0C, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },

        // ---------- 37-38  synth pad ----------

        // Warm pad. The slowest attack in the bank — AR 3 takes most of a
        // second to arrive, which is what a pad is for.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1E, m_atk = 0x32, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x61, c_scale = 0x05, c_atk = 0x43, c_sus = 0x09, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x01 },
        // Bright pad. Faster and more present, with a half-sine carrier.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x61, m_scale = 0x1A, m_atk = 0x43, m_sus = 0x09, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x04, c_atk = 0x54, c_sus = 0x0A, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x01 },

        // ---------- 39-43  effects, ethnic, percussive, orchestral ----------

        // Synth effects. 7:1 with an abs-sine modulator, slow and
        // inharmonic — atmosphere rather than a note.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x67, m_scale = 0x1A, m_atk = 0x53, m_sus = 0x14, m_wave = 0x02,
            c_char = 0x21, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0x64, c_sus = 0x15, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x00 },
        // Plucked ethnic — sitar, banjo, shamisen, koto. Bright, buzzy,
        // fast decay: half-sine on both and heavy feedback.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x46, m_atk = 0xF7, m_sus = 0x07, m_wave = 0x01,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF6, c_sus = 0x06, c_wave = 0x01,
            n_conn = 0x0C },
        // Tuned percussion — agogo, steel drum, woodblock, melodic tom.
        // Metallic 5:1 with a very fast decay.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x05, m_scale = 0x4A, m_atk = 0xF9, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF8, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x02 },
        // Sound effects. A wildly inharmonic 15:14 pair on pulse-sine, which
        // has no pitch left in it to speak of — which is the point.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0F, m_scale = 0x45, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0E, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x06 },
        // Timpani. Low, round, and slow to fade — pitched, unlike the kit
        // drums below, because a MIDI file plays it as notes.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x0E, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x06, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x05, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06 },

        // ---------- 44-59  percussion ----------
        //
        // Every one of these sets `fixed_note`: a drum's pitch belongs to the
        // drum, and the note number on channel 9 chose *which* drum rather
        // than how high it sounds. Noisy voices (snare, hats, cymbals) get
        // there through a large, deliberately inharmonic MULT ratio on
        // pulse-sine waveforms — the OPL2 has no noise generator, so an
        // unrelated ratio high in the spectrum is the closest thing to one.

        // Kick. Low and short, almost pure sine. A real kick sweeps its
        // pitch down as it decays and this cannot, so it leans on the
        // envelope alone.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x08, m_atk = 0xF9, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF8, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x08, fixed_note = 26 },
        // Snare. 12:14 on pulse-sine with heavy feedback — the closest this
        // chip gets to a band of noise — cut short.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0C, m_scale = 0x48, m_atk = 0xF9, m_sus = 0x09, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0E, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF9, c_sus = 0x08, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0C, fixed_note = 60 },
        // Side stick. The same noise recipe an octave up and shorter still,
        // so it reads as a click on the rim rather than a hit on the head.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0D, m_scale = 0x4C, m_atk = 0xFB, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0F, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xFA, c_sus = 0x09, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0A, fixed_note = 72 },
        // Toms: one patch each, identical but for the note they sound. Three
        // entries rather than one is what `fixed_note` costs — and what
        // buys a kit whose toms are actually at different pitches.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x0C, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06, fixed_note = 36 },
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x0C, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06, fixed_note = 43 },
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x01, m_scale = 0x0C, m_atk = 0xF8, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF7, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06, fixed_note = 50 },
        // Closed hi-hat. The fastest release of the metal voices, because a
        // hat that rings sounds open — but only just fast enough. At RR 11
        // it decayed 48 dB in fourteen milliseconds, which is a click with a
        // high peak and almost no energy: it measured 24 dB under the rest
        // of the kit and vanished in any mix. RR 9 is 60 ms, still tight.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0F, m_scale = 0x4A, m_atk = 0xFC, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0E, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xFB, c_sus = 0x09, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0E, fixed_note = 84 },
        // Open hi-hat. Same voice, allowed to decay.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0F, m_scale = 0x4A, m_atk = 0xF6, m_sus = 0x07, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0E, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF5, c_sus = 0x06, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0E, fixed_note = 84 },
        // Crash. The longest decay of the metal voices, and deliberately
        // three decibels under the rest of the kit: a cymbal that rings for
        // two seconds at snare level buries the bar it lands in.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0F, m_scale = 0x48, m_atk = 0xF4, m_sus = 0x05, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0D, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF3, c_sus = 0x04, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0E, fixed_note = 80 },
        // Ride. Higher, tighter and more pitched than the crash — an
        // abs-sine carrier keeps a little of the bell's ping.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0E, m_scale = 0x4B, m_atk = 0xF5, m_sus = 0x06, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0C, c_scale = 0x03, c_atk = 0xF4, c_sus = 0x05, c_wave = 0x02,
            n_conn = 0x0C, fixed_note = 88 },
        // Hand clap. A short mid-band burst.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0D, m_scale = 0x49, m_atk = 0xFA, m_sus = 0x09, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0F, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF9, c_sus = 0x08, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0C, fixed_note = 66 },
        // Cowbell and agogo. Pitched metal, 7:5 — inharmonic enough to
        // clang, harmonic enough to have a note.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x07, m_scale = 0x4E, m_atk = 0xFA, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x05, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF9, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x08, fixed_note = 76 },
        // Woodblock and claves. 3:1 sine, very short — wood is a pitch with
        // no sustain rather than a noise.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x03, m_scale = 0x4B, m_atk = 0xFC, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xFB, c_sus = 0x09, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x04, fixed_note = 82 },
        // Tambourine. High jingle, medium decay.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0F, m_scale = 0x4C, m_atk = 0xFA, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0D, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF9, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0C, fixed_note = 90 },
        // Congas, bongos and timbales, all as one hand drum. A skin struck
        // with a hand: pitched, quick, no metal.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x02, m_scale = 0x0E, m_atk = 0xF9, m_sus = 0x08, m_wave = 0x00,
            c_char = 0x01, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xF8, c_sus = 0x07, c_wave = 0x00,
            n_conn = 0x06, fixed_note = 52 },
        // The catch-all for whistles, guiros, cuicas and triangles: a quiet
        // short tick that keeps a rhythm without pretending to be the
        // instrument it stands in for.
        opl_midi.patch {
            m_char = 0x0C, m_scale = 0x50, m_atk = 0xFB, m_sus = 0x0A, m_wave = 0x03,
            c_char = 0x0E, c_scale = 0x00, c_atk = 0xFA, c_sus = 0x09, c_wave = 0x03,
            n_conn = 0x0A, fixed_note = 78 } })!

        let mel = alloc(i32[128] {})!
        let drm = alloc(i32[128] {})!
        for i in 0..128 do
            mel[i] = program_map[i]
            drm[i] = -1
        for i in 0..(drum_map.len / 2i64) do
            drm[drum_map[i * 2i64]] = drum_map[i * 2i64 + 1i64]

        let rk = rhythm_kit()
        let rmap = alloc(i32[128] {})!
        for i in 0..128 do
            rmap[i] = -1
        for i in 0..(rhythm_notes.len / 2i64) do
            rmap[rhythm_notes[i * 2i64]] = rhythm_notes[i * 2i64 + 1i64]

        opl_midi.bank { name = "fc-gm", patches = ps, melodic = mel, drums = drm,
                        rhythm = rk, rhythm_map = rmap }
